# Evidence — repository protection state (EP-00 criterion C8)

Captured: 2026-11-06. Read-only GitHub REST reads through the authenticated GitHub CLI.
No settings were changed; no protection attempt was made beyond the read.

---

## 1. Repository metadata

```text
$ gh repo view Oludammy2030/Hivorr --json visibility,defaultBranchRef,isPrivate
{"defaultBranchRef":{"name":"main"},"isPrivate":true,"visibility":"PRIVATE"}
```

## 2. Classic branch protection

```text
$ gh api repos/Oludammy2030/Hivorr/branches/main/protection
{"message":"Upgrade to GitHub Pro or make this repository public to enable this feature.",
 "documentation_url":"https://docs.github.com/rest/branches/branch-protection#get-branch-protection",
 "status":"403"}
gh: Upgrade to GitHub Pro or make this repository public to enable this feature. (HTTP 403)
```

## 3. Interpretation

- C8 is **unmet**: the host refuses protection on a private repository on the current plan.
- The refusal is a plan capability, not a configuration defect. It cannot be fixed from the
  repository, a workflow or a hook.
- The compensating control in force is documented in
  `documents/Engineering-Execution/BRANCH_PROTECTION.md`: a committed local pre-push hook
  (`.githooks/pre-push` → `scripts/pre-push.mjs`), a CI-side `verify-pr` guard
  (`.github/workflows/branch-guard.yml` → `scripts/verify-pr-merge.mjs`), and the deployment
  gates that depend on it.
- `BRANCH_PROTECTION.md` §6 states the limits: a user with write/admin access can push through
  the API or another clone, can force-push, and the CI guard detects a bypass only after it
  happened. The control is defence in depth against accidental pushes, **not** server-side
  protection.
- Clearing C8 requires a founder decision: upgrade to GitHub Pro (paid), approve a change of
  host, or approve a visibility change. Decision #18 currently fixes a private repository, so
  the visibility route contradicts a recorded founder decision and cannot be taken by an agent.
